\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces ADM masses of the spherical stars against oscillation frequency, from Eqs.~\eqref  {eq:starode} at the working couplings. Lower $\omega $ means deeper binding and larger $|M_{\rm  ADM}|$; in the shaded region the solver finds no dressed star. At equal frequency the phantom star is the heavier in magnitude ($20.3\%$ at $\omega =0.550$), its repulsive self-gravity spreading it over more volume. Filled stars: the reference pair, both sectors at $\omega =0.550$. Open star: the equal-$|M_{\rm  ADM}|$ variant, phantom retuned to $\omega =0.56598$ so that $M_-$ matches the canonical mass to one part in $10^5$.}}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:family}{{2}{3}{ADM masses of the spherical stars against oscillation frequency, from Eqs.~\eqref {eq:starode} at the working couplings. Lower $\omega $ means deeper binding and larger $|M_{\rm ADM}|$; in the shaded region the solver finds no dressed star. At equal frequency the phantom star is the heavier in magnitude ($20.3\%$ at $\omega =0.550$), its repulsive self-gravity spreading it over more volume. Filled stars: the reference pair, both sectors at $\omega =0.550$. Open star: the equal-$|M_{\rm ADM}|$ variant, phantom retuned to $\omega =0.56598$ so that $M_-$ matches the canonical mass to one part in $10^5$}{figure.2}{}}
