\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The convergence study. (a)~The reference cell's resolution ladder in time (dotted $\Delta x=0.50$, dashed $0.33$, solid $0.25$) against the \texttt  {PP} control's ladder: the mixed-pair curves lie on top of one another while the control's residual drift roughly halves at every refinement step. (b)~The same data as a refinement sequence---$|\Delta \bar  {x}|$ at $t=60$ against grid spacing, grid refined to the right---for every family. The mixed pairs sit on plateaus (spread $0.5\%$ at $d_0=8$, $6.3\%$ at $d_0=12$) while the same-sign controls fall towards zero. The under-resolved $d_0=16$ family is the exception that shows the test works: its $53\%$ spread is why it is quoted as a bound.}}{8}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:convergence}{{6}{8}{The convergence study. (a)~The reference cell's resolution ladder in time (dotted $\Delta x=0.50$, dashed $0.33$, solid $0.25$) against the \texttt {PP} control's ladder: the mixed-pair curves lie on top of one another while the control's residual drift roughly halves at every refinement step. (b)~The same data as a refinement sequence---$|\Delta \bar {x}|$ at $t=60$ against grid spacing, grid refined to the right---for every family. The mixed pairs sit on plateaus (spread $0.5\%$ at $d_0=8$, $6.3\%$ at $d_0=12$) while the same-sign controls fall towards zero. The under-resolved $d_0=16$ family is the exception that shows the test works: its $53\%$ spread is why it is quoted as a bound}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {III}{\ignorespaces The runaway against matched point-mass Bondi integrations: two point particles carrying the stars' ADM masses, released at rest at the same separation, each accelerated by the \emph  {other's} active mass. ``NR'' is the simulation and ``model'' the integration. Left: the phantom's drift in the reference cell, measured and modelled. Right: measured over modelled phantom drift for each separation, all at $\Delta x=0.25$. The phantom sector carries the comparison because the canonical centroid is biased low by the halo (Sec.~\ref {sec:caveats}); the clean window is $t\lesssim 30$, and the $d_0=16$ entries bound rather than measure (Sec.~\ref {sec:convergence}).}}{8}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:pointmass}{{III}{8}{The runaway against matched point-mass Bondi integrations: two point particles carrying the stars' ADM masses, released at rest at the same separation, each accelerated by the \emph {other's} active mass. ``NR'' is the simulation and ``model'' the integration. Left: the phantom's drift in the reference cell, measured and modelled. Right: measured over modelled phantom drift for each separation, all at $\Delta x=0.25$. The phantom sector carries the comparison because the canonical centroid is biased low by the halo (Sec.~\ref {sec:caveats}); the clean window is $t\lesssim 30$, and the $d_0=16$ entries bound rather than measure (Sec.~\ref {sec:convergence})}{table.3}{}}
